  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Friday)\nIssued at 1057 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nLatest extended range forecast data suggest that a weak northern \nstream trough will advance slowly eastward from the Mid-MS Valley \ninto the northern Mid-Atlantic states next week, as it will likely\nbe blocked by ridging to its north. At the surface, a trough \n(initially extending from central parts of MS/AL north- \nnortheastward into the Upper OH Valley) is predicted to remain \nnearly stationary on Tuesday, and will likely focus development of\nadditional showers and thunderstorms across our region both \nduring the day and at night. In similar fashion to Monday, a few \nstrong-severe thunderstorms may occur during the afternoon and \nevening hours, aided by high CAPE and increasing W-NW flow aloft.\n\nAlthough there is some uncertainty regarding precisely when this \nwill occur, the surface trough is expected to shift slowly \nsoutheastward at some point Wednesday/Thursday, allowing a \ncooler/drier airmass (originating from an anticyclone across \nsouthern QC) to spread southeastward into the region. This coupled\nwith 20-30 knot W-NW flow aloft and dry advection through the \ndepth of the tropospheric column will allow rain chances to \ndecrease considerably on Thursday/Friday. Afternoon highs will \nfall back into the l-m 80s by the end of the work week, with \novernight lows Thursday night/Friday morning in the u50s-l60s.",
